---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title =
  "Fidelic: run your Salesforce integration tests without waiting for a sandbox.";
const description =
  "Salesforce integration tests that run on your laptop and in CI — no org, no login, nothing shared. Fidelic is a 10 MB container that loads your real schema and runs your real Apex triggers, so the tests run on every push instead of whenever an environment frees up.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  {/* SECTION 1 — the problem. SANDBOX ELIMINATION IS THE PRODUCT; emulation is HOW it
      works and has been demoted to section 2 accordingly. The old hero led with "no real CI",
      which names a category rather than the thing a reader is currently waiting on.

      EVERY CLAIM ON THIS PAGE TRACES TO A COMMAND, per the standing rule. The table is here so
      the next person editing this copy can re-run them instead of trusting them:

        "without waiting for a sandbox"  → docs/ci/github-actions.md; real workflow run
                                           35263998942, 16s, 4/4 tests, no org involved
        "on every push"                  → same run, triggered by push
        "10 MB"                          → hub.docker.com/r/fidelic/emulator, 10.34 MB compressed
        "starts in under a second"       → docker run … --apex-src ./mdapi → 0.29s to listening
                                           (8 triggers). A 1,500-class org takes ~15s; boot is
                                           proportional to how much Apex you have, and QUICKSTART
                                           §3 says so rather than this page implying otherwise.
        "about two milliseconds"         → POST /__emulator__/reset, median 1.9ms over HTTP, n=20
        "side by side"                   → two containers on different ports: a record created in
                                           one leaves the other at totalSize 0
        "row locks, rate limits"         → POST /__emulator__/faults; conformance faults-001..005
        "your real Apex triggers"        → GET /__emulator__/fired, outcome entered-clean

      FINDINGS #18 review: no count of triggers that RUN appears on this page. The only trigger
      count is in the playground section, which says "loaded and classified" and is unchanged. */}
  <h1>Run your Salesforce integration tests without waiting for a sandbox.</h1>

  <p class="sub">
    Sandboxes are scarce, shared, and slow to refresh. Tests queue for an environment,
    then fail because someone else changed the data underneath them. So the tests that
    would catch the most expensive bugs are the ones that get run least.
  </p>

  {/* SECTION 2 — the mechanism. Emulation is the HOW. */}
  <h2>It runs your org on your own machine, so nothing queues.</h2>

  <p class="answer">
    Fidelic is a 10 MB container that loads your org's real schema and runs your real Apex
    triggers. Your tests point at <code>localhost</code> and get the same REST responses, the
    same trigger side effects, the same validation errors. It starts in under a second on a
    small org, resets to empty in about two milliseconds, and runs as many copies side by side
    as you have cores — so the same suite runs on your laptop and on every push in CI.
  </p>

  {/* The CI claim above must be reachable, not merely asserted: this is the recipe that was
      executed as a real workflow run before it was published. */}
  <p class="answer">
    <a href="/docs/ci-github-actions">Copy the CI recipe</a> —
    a GitHub Actions workflow that boots the org, waits until your Apex has actually loaded,
    runs your tests and tears down.
  </p>

  <p class="answer">
    It can also fail on purpose. One call arms a row lock, a rate limit, or slow responses,
    and the next request comes back with the real Salesforce error. A sandbox hands you
    those when it feels like it and never when you ask — which is why the error handling
    meant to catch them has usually never run.
  </p>

  {/* TRANSCRIPT, NOT A COMPOSITION — every line below is verbatim program output.
      Captured 2026-09-16 from image cdcd3ae against NPSP @ 19646faa, force-app subtree
      (26 triggers, 1,503 classes), on the production box, a Hetzner CPX21 with 3 vCPU.

      MOUNT force-app, NOT THE REPO ROOT. The first version of this block mounted ./npsp
      while showing output captured from ./npsp/force-app, so the commands and the output
      disagreed and nobody could tell: the repo root carries 9 more classes that live
      outside the deployable source and parse with errors, giving 1,514 classes and
      "3 with ERROR nodes" instead of 1,503 and zero. force-app is what the project's own
      corpus definition points at (demo.Corpus.ApexSubdir).

      Reproduce both commands exactly:
        docker run --rm -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro --entrypoint /sfapex \
          fidelic/emulator:cdcd3ae coverage /mdapi
        docker run -p 8080:8080 -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro -e SFEMU_FAULT_LOCK_ON_UPDATE=true \
          fidelic/emulator:cdcd3ae --apex-src /mdapi

      Long lines are elided with … and nothing is reworded. The one line that is OURS
      rather than the program's is the 15.9s timing, marked with a # as a comment,
      because sfemu does not print a boot duration. If re-running stops reproducing
      this, the copy is stale — that is the point of naming the commit.

      THE BUILD STAMP IS ELIDED ON PURPOSE. The boot line really reads
      `sfemu <sha> listening on :8080 …`, and that sha changes on every release, so
      writing one here would date this page the moment the next image ships — the same
      trap that left "v1.0" on this line for months. The commit above is the provenance;
      the … is where its stamp was. Everything either side of it is byte-for-byte what
      the program printed, re-verified against the live image on 2026-09-16.

      The previous version of this block was a composition: it showed analyzer output
      under an emulator command, invented trigger names, and a REFUSED verdict the
      product has never emitted. */}
  <div
    class="terminal"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Terminal transcript: the sfapex analyzer classifies the Nonprofit Success Pack's 26 triggers at load time as 7 EXECUTE, 19 UNRESOLVED; the emulator then boots that org in 15.9 seconds and, with failure injection enabled, a PATCH returns HTTP 400 with an UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW error array."
  >
    <pre><span class="dim"># what will run, before running it</span>
<span class="cmd">docker run --rm -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro --entrypoint /sfapex fidelic/emulator coverage /mdapi</span>
COVERAGE
  parsed:   1061 files (0 with ERROR nodes, 0 error nodes total)
  found:    26 trigger(s), 1503 class(es)

RUNG B LADDER @ C1 + C2 families &#123;…&#125; <span class="dim">(load-time classification)</span>: EXECUTE=7  HOOKS=0  UNRESOLVED=19  RECORDED-ONLY=0  (SIMULATED=1)
  <span class="ok">EXECUTE</span>     TDTM_DataImportBatch
  <span class="sim">EXECUTE*</span>    TDTM_FormTemplate  (SIMULATED: settings: Gift_Entry_Settings__c.getInstance — Custom Setting read at C2)
  <span class="bad">UNRESOLVED</span>  TDTM_HouseholdObject  (min tier C3; blocked by dispatch: interface Evaluator [C3])

<span class="dim"># now boot it, with the row-lock fault armed</span>
<span class="cmd">docker run -p 8080:8080 -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro -e SFEMU_FAULT_LOCK_ON_UPDATE=true fidelic/emulator --apex-src /mdapi</span>
sfemu: apexexec ON — 26 triggers loaded from "/mdapi", 7 EXECUTE-classified
sfemu … listening on :8080 (store: sqlite ":memory:", client-secret: false, metadata: "/mdapi", hooks-file: "")
<span class="dim"># 14.1s to listening — NPSP, 26 triggers / 1,503 classes, 3-vCPU cloud VM</span>

<span class="cmd">curl -sX PATCH localhost:8080/services/data/v62.0/sobjects/Account/001000000000038AAA \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '&#123;"Rating":"Hot"&#125;'</span>
<span class="bad">HTTP 400</span>
<span class="bad">[&#123;"message":"unable to obtain exclusive access to this record or 1 records",</span>
<span class="bad">  "errorCode":"UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW","fields":[]&#125;]</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="answer">
    Before anything runs, every trigger in your org gets one of four verdicts, so you know
    what you are looking at before you trust a result:
  </p>

  <ul class="answer-list">
    <li>
      <strong>EXECUTE</strong> — nothing in the reachable code is unsupported, so the
      interpreter will attempt your real Apex. That is a prediction, not a receipt: the
      fired log tells you what actually ran.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>HOOKS</strong> — approximated. The effect is reproduced by a rule instead of
      by running the Apex, and it is labelled as such rather than passed off as the real thing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>RECORDED-ONLY</strong> — the code could run, but the thing that starts it
      cannot happen on your machine — a platform event, for instance. Nothing is claimed
      for it either way.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>UNRESOLVED</strong> — refused, naming the file, the line, and the construct
      it uses that we do not support. A refusal you can read beats a wrong answer you cannot see.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="answer">
    Classified is not the same as executed, and the analyzer never claims otherwise.
    The same four words appear in your own report and in the emulator's boot output —
    <a href="/capabilities#vocabulary">see exactly what we support</a>, capability by
    capability, generated from the classifier itself.
  </p>

  {/* SECTION 2b — the live playground.
      A LINK AND A REAL TRANSCRIPT, not an iframe. An iframe would load the whole
      playground app into this page for every visitor who never clicks it, which is
      third-party JavaScript in the critical path of a site that currently ships none.
      The transcript below is verbatim output from a real session against the live
      box — not a mock-up — so it stays honest without costing a single byte of image. */}
  <div class="tryit">
    <h2>Or run it now, in your browser.</h2>

    <p class="answer">
      {/* FINDINGS #18: no artifact may state or imply that N triggers RUN. This line
          previously read "26 triggers, 7 of them executing real Apex" — but 7 is the
          CLASSIFIED-EXECUTE count, and runtime verification puts 2 of those 7 entered
          and 5 refused. "Loaded and classified" is what the scanner knows; "one of its
          real handlers fires" is what corpus-exec and the playground's own condition 7
          actually verify. Claim the verified thing. */}
      <a href="https://play.fidelic.dev">play.fidelic.dev</a> hands you a real
      NPSP org with the actual open-source nonprofit codebase. All 26 triggers in that repo
      are loaded and classified. Click a command and watch one of its real handlers fire, then
      throw the org away. No install, no signup, no Salesforce login.
    </p>

    <div
      class="terminal"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Playground transcript: creating a DataImportBatch record returns HTTP 201, and the fired-trigger log shows TDTM_DataImportBatch running on both before-insert and after-insert."
    >
      <pre><span class="accent">POST /services/data/v62.0/sobjects/DataImportBatch__c</span> <span class="dim">&#123;"Name":"Spring Appeal"&#125;</span>
<span class="ok">201</span>  <span class="dim">&#123;"id":"a0400000000006zAAA","success":true&#125;</span>

<span class="accent">GET /__emulator__/fired</span>
  <span class="ok">TDTM_DataImportBatch</span>  <span class="dim">before insert</span>  entered-clean
  <span class="ok">TDTM_DataImportBatch</span>  <span class="dim">after  insert</span>  entered-clean</pre>
    </div>

    <p>
      <a class="btn" href="https://play.fidelic.dev">Open the playground →</a>
    </p>

    {/* Says what is true rather than what sells. The pool is small and finite, and a
        visitor who meets the busy path should have been told it exists. */}
    <p class="soon">
      A handful of sandboxes run at a time. If they are all busy you will be given
      the command to run it yourself instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* SECTION 3 — receipts, proof, CTA */}
  <div class="receipts">
    <p>
      Verified 68/68 against the public conformance suite
      <a href="https://github.com/fidelic-dev/fidelic-conformance"
        >run the scorecard yourself →</a
      >
    </p>
    <p class="soon">
      68/68 pass · 5 skipped (need a real org) · 3 pending (awaiting real-org
      verification). The skips and pendings are the methodology showing its
      work.
    </p>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="pullquote">
    <p>
      The failure injection alone is worth it. Row locks and API limits are
      untestable in any real org. Fidelic makes them a config flag.
    </p>
    <cite>— Engineer at a major Salesforce partner</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="waitlist" id="waitlist">
    <h2>Get early access</h2>
    <p>
      We onboard a few teams at a time. Real CI for your Salesforce stack starts
      with an email.
    </p>
    <form action="https://formspree.io/f/mkodpozp" method="POST">
